00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:06.869 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  andy is 22 years old  works as a personal trainer in shanghai  and is transgender  once a month or so he gets a delivery  from thailand  and it brings a risk  [Music]  these are male hormone drugs which do  not have approval in china  [Music]  for many trans people buying medicine in  the grey market makes more sense than  through the limited official channels  buying drugs unofficially can be  dangerous with unscrupulous  [Music]  trans people administering their own  medication have experienced kidney  problems  and even paralysis as a result  they take those risks because in china  they exist in the margins  being transgender is officially classed  as a mental illness  there are three important bits of  context to all of this and the first  is the government side the laws and the  regulations in 2017  china passed a law saying that if you  have gender affirming surgery  you can change your identity on national  documents identity cards things like  that which is important  for many trans people but it doesn't  make it easy first you have to get that  diagnosis of being transgender which is  still officially  a mental illness that diagnosis itself  takes a year  and before that point you have to have  lived five years in your gender  then you still need to get your parents  consent whether you're 18 years old  whether you're 50 years old  you still need to get your parents to  sign off on that so all of that makes it  really difficult to enter the system  on the other hand that doesn't stop some  people from going through that official  process  in beijing mr c is going through the  official channels  um mr c is one of the most visible trans  men in china  after winning a court case against  workplace discrimination this summer  but he's had to fight his whole life  the second part of this is not what the  government does but it's about chinese  society's attitudes in general towards  trans people  now there's no uniformity here china has  a long tradition in its history and it's  myth  of gender diversity and in modern times  there's jin singh she is a famous  celebrity  who used to be a soldier in the people's  liberation army before she had sexual  surgery in the 90s  and a lot of people know her story but  there is a flip side  too that jin shing doesn't talk about  trans rights she doesn't try to speak  for the trans community  and throughout society at large in  eastern nature it can be  very conservative gender fluidity is  still viewed with a deep suspicion in  many places  mr c believes the only way for trans  people to be treated better  to avoid the risks many of them  currently run in the shadows  is to be more visible  foreign the last bit of context  is civil society things like ngos which  can often move  issues forward there did used to be a  lot of these for lgbt issues in china  especially in the 90s  and 2000s but since 2012 there has been  a crackdown on them not  specifically on lgbt issues but on ngos  generally china has a deep suspicion  of citizens organizing themselves and  there's less space generally  for those sort of issues to get a  hearing shanghai pride had been running  for a decade but this summer it was  closed without any reason given so a lot  of people  in the community feel like they are  being targeted they're being  sent a message that these topics are not  up for discussion in the public sphere  so without those avenues it's really  relying on individuals speaking up  speaking to the media to try and make a  difference to try and change attitudes  andy too wants his existence and his  community  to be better seen um  for now that future is something to look  to not  to live

